{"title":"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In a drawer Sandy has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> pairs of socks, each pair a different color.  On Monday Sandy selects two individual socks at random from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> socks in the drawer.  On Tuesday Sandy selects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of the remaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> socks at random and on Wednesday two of the remaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> socks at random.  The probability that Wednesday is the first day Sandy selects matching socks is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers,  Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The order of the days doesn't matter, therefore we can solve backwards. \n</p><p>Let the fifth sock be arbitrary; the probability that the sixth sock matches in color is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0113e5c4f21b056d984cf055365370cbf0e66dae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. \n</p><p>Assuming this, then let the first sock be arbitrary; the probability that the second sock does not match is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/0/3007bf67be3b5f5b5288ff0a73ff839bd421d267.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{6}{7}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"16\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>The only \"hard\" part is the third and fourth sock. But that is simple casework. If the third sock's color matches the color of one of the first two socks (which occurs with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/f/a0f1fce186d69bf5e1b835be3d54d901ac6142d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"52\" height=\"38\" />), then the fourth sock can be arbitrary. Otherwise (with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/3/8031bb3f1dac63198f00d4e91a0459672bd47a07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />), the fourth sock can be chosen with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/1/af171b50efff5981bffa32b6903c7aa601c8ed7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{4}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> (5 socks left, 1 sock that can possibly match the third sock's color). The desired probability is thus\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/7/d37377aab06030880139cc6f8d25eaf937c7b7f7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) = \\frac{26}{315}.\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" />\nThe sum is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/3/ff3e6ac209d0a2f31bee6802de6b52826df2526c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26+315=\\boxed{341}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The key is to count backwards. First, choose the pair which you pick on Wednesday in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways. Then there are four pairs of socks for you to pick a pair of on Tuesday, and you don't want to pick a pair. Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> pairs, the number of ways to do this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/4/b94e343f1a18b323216d5ddcb177ce18788e18f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom{8}{2}-4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"68\" height=\"53\" />. Then, there are two pairs and two nonmatching socks for you to pick from on Monday, a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> socks. Since you don't want to pick a pair, the number of ways to do this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c617633de16e0dc1a8708e35691a1a036947c17b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom{6}{2}-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"68\" height=\"53\" />. Thus the answer is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/c/95cd0ea855b67859a8b01759503cd7963ca12ba6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{\\left(5\\right)\\left(\\dbinom{8}{2}-4\\right)\\left(\\dbinom{6}{2}-2\\right)}{\\dbinom{10}{2}\\dbinom{8}{2}\\dbinom{6}{2}}=\\dfrac{26}{315}.\\]\" width=\"283\" height=\"112\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/0/0a07283ed87fbb63b54b818828f3e7dde2f980a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26 + 315 = \\boxed{341}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"131\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For the first sock, note that to pick two different socks, can complementary count to get the total, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f99361492168635014ea996bc48228c5ed8afae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{10}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"42\" height=\"53\" /> minus the number of pairs (5) to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/3/2f34ca874826f6e3d8da2c0029e8babd49050b33.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\binom{10}{2} - 5}{\\binom{10}{2}}\\]\" width=\"61\" height=\"49\" />\nThe next steps aren't quite as simple, though.\nWLOG suppose the socks are (a, a, b, b, c, c, d, d, e, e) and that we chose ab on the first day.\nThis leaves abccddee as the remaining socks. We can think of our next choice as how many \"pairs\" we \"break\".\nNote that right now, in abccddee there are 3 pairs, namely {cc, dd, ee}. Our next choice could either leave all of these pairs (if we choose ab), leave 2 of these pairs, (choose say ac to keep dd and ee as pairs), or leave only 1 as a pair, say choosing de to get abccde.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/3/9d37494edad4f95c668630a4ed356840767bb1e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\bold{\\text{Case 1: Break zero pairs}}.\\]\" width=\"181\" height=\"15\" /> In this case, we want to keep 3 pairs remaining, so our only choice is to choose AB, which would make the remaining ccddee. The probability that we choose AB is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c86cf36d33273a85fa2a4a40e94c30bfd1718f78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\binom{8}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"29\" height=\"46\" /> and the probability that we get a pair after this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0facda83c06a3b85a5114f090c499742522879e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{\\binom{6}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"29\" height=\"46\" /> since we just choose CC, DD, or EE. This case has a probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/1/811f0621d04db206ad1491928de75335d98f95fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{3}{\\binom{6}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"61\" height=\"46\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/5/3b51ac0bf7572b2d838cf305657be9e22a3db46f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\bold{\\text{Case 2: Break one pair}}.\\]\" width=\"170\" height=\"15\" />\nIn this case, we want to choose A and one of {C,C,D,D,E,E} or B and one of {C,C,D,D,E,E}. This yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c70f8440dd86d7c7bd7d53b6be79c3bcd4416eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6 + 6}{\\binom{8}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"46\" height=\"46\" /> probability. After this, we end up with 2 pairs, so the total probability of choosing a pair on Wednesday in this case is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/e/a0e726bff2fd81d6586a475d1cd498907b683a97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6 + 6}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{2}{\\binom{6}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"78\" height=\"46\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de20158a646730280f9547f2865cb5cd9a59acc8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\bold{\\text{case 3: Break 2 pairs}}.\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"15\" />\nIn this case we'd choose two distinct characters from {C,C,D,D,E,E} which would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/b/28b10b2e89f9b5e334973926284c956309a41c0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\binom{6}{2} - 3}{\\binom{6}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"65\" height=\"51\" /> After this, only one pair remains so the total probability for this case is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/f/fdfc69af3bab4d80e66b3667193ea3b4c495adad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\binom{6}{2} - 3}{\\binom{6}{2}}    \\frac{6}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{1}{\\binom{6}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"119\" height=\"51\" />\nAdding all the cases and multiplying by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/d/18dd84c251bea724a9724eedc76912a6f0277361.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\binom{10}{2} - 5}{\\binom{10}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"67\" height=\"51\" /> yields\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/9/8691c698ec86cfb9f070e3cb0ee6270250ca7050.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\binom{10}{2} - 5}{\\binom{10}{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{3}{\\binom{6}{2}}+\\frac{6 + 6}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{2}{\\binom{6}{2}}+ \\frac{\\binom{6}{2} - 3}{\\binom{6}{2}}\\frac{6}{\\binom{8}{2}}\\frac{1}{\\binom{6}{2}}\\right) = \\frac{26}{315} \\rightarrow 26 + 315 = \\boxed{341}\\]\" width=\"582\" height=\"53\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem is very very similar to <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_UNCO_Math_Contest_II_Problems/Problem_7\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_UNCO_Math_Contest_II_Problems/Problem_7</a>. I will use the results I derived there.\nWe have already established that the probability we get a mismatch on Wednesday GIVEN that there is a mismatch on Monday, Tuesday is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/8/e38412a49c40fd206ba1aa472172062f902c0e17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{112}{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"37\" />, meaning the probability for match is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/8/a88781472565e94336ebdb87a480af4b3b6fb32a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{13}{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. Now, we find the probability that Monday,Tuesday are a mismatch and product it with this.\nMonday: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c6f2f1e29fae4fdba14c042237c4634b1f7484.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 - \\frac{5}{\\binom{10}{2}} = \\frac{8}{9}\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"45\" />\nTuesday: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/f/96fcdec397ef1934a6282da0881197ab65a17e2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 - \\frac{3}{\\binom{8}{2}} = \\frac{25}{28}\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"45\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/4/ec49c05152d7985a41b9d99400db6235bfb4dfbc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(\\text{ Monday,Tuesday mismatch AND Wednesday match}) = P(\\text{ Monday,Tuesday mismatch)} \\cdot P(\\text{Wednesday match given Monday,Tuesday mismatch})\\]\" width=\"1073\" height=\"18\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/1/fc1a429b9c9ce7c7a259543d7e309ce4759c4908.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies P = \\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\frac{25}{28} \\cdot \\frac{13}{125} = \\boxed{\\frac{26}{315}}\\]\" width=\"236\" height=\"48\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I\" title=\"2015 AIME I\">2015 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2015 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2015\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2015 AIME I Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017235425\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.040 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.061 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 299/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1786/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 108/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8671/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.401      1 -total\n 61.19%    8.200      1 Template:AIME_box\n 36.55%    4.898      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:14098:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017235425 and revision id 259348. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}